@[expose] public section

universe u

/-!
# Strict henselization is compatible with quotients

Let `(R, 𝔪, κ)` be a local ring, let `R → Rsh` be a strict henselization of `R` — henselian local,
ind-étale over `R`, with maximal ideal generated by `𝔪` and residue field a separable algebraic
closure `κ^{sep}` of `κ` — and let `I ⊆ 𝔪` be an ideal. This file proves that `Rsh/I Rsh` is a
strict henselization of `R/I`.

The argument is the source's: the case `S = R/I` of Lemma 05WR. Indeed `R/I` is local, so it is
its own localization at `𝔪/I`, and `R` is its own localization at `𝔪`; the ring map `R → R/I` is
surjective, hence of finite type and quasi-finite; the base change of `R/I` along `R → Rsh` is
`Rsh/I Rsh`; the separable algebraic closure `κ₂` of the residue field of `R/I` may be taken to be
`κ^{sep}` itself, since `R → R/I` induces an isomorphism `κ ≅ κ(R/I)`; and with that choice the
map `π : Rsh/I Rsh → κ₂` of Lemma 05WR is the one induced by `Rsh → κ^{sep}`, whose kernel is the
maximal ideal of `Rsh/I Rsh`, so that `Rsh/I Rsh` is its own localization at `ker π`. Lemma 05WR
then produces the four properties of a strict henselization of `R/I` for that ring.

## Main results

* `Stacks.StrictHenselization.QuotientDescent.solution`: the statement of Stacks Tag 05WS.

## Implementation notes

The source's phrase "the separable algebraic closure of `κ(R/I)` may be taken to be `κ^{sep}`" is
the step of the specialization that carries mathematical content, and that content is about fields
alone: `Stacks.StrictHenselization.QuotientDescent.exists_isSepClosure_of_ringEquiv` says that a
separable algebraic closure `M` of a field `K` is a separable algebraic closure of every field `L`
isomorphic to `K`, for the structure map transported along the isomorphism `e : K ≃+* L`, and it
returns that structure map together with the identity `(L → M) ∘ e = (K → M)` which pins it down.
This is the separable analogue of `IsAlgClosed.of_ringEquiv`, and like it needs only one transport
lemma, `Algebra.IsSeparable.of_equiv_equiv`: `IsSepClosed M` is a property of `M` alone, so only
separability of `M` over the base moves.

What this proof uses is that lemma applied to the isomorphism of residue fields induced by a
surjective local ring map,
`Stacks.StrictHenselization.QuotientDescent.residueField_map_bijective_of_surjective`, packaged with
the square it makes commute over the map itself, `κ(B) → M` after `B → κ(B)` after `f` being `κ(A) →
M` after `A → κ(A)`:
`Stacks.StrictHenselization.QuotientDescent.exists_isSepClosure_residueField_of_surjective`. Both
halves of the specialization come out of that one statement — the instance `IsSepClosure
(ResidueField (R/I)) κ^{sep}` demanded by Lemma 05WR, and its hypothesis `hπSq`, which is that
square transported through `Ideal.Quotient.ringHom_ext`. Surjectivity of `f` is its only hypothesis
on `f`: locality is a consequence, `IsLocalHom.of_surjective`.

The map `π` of Lemma 05WR is `Ideal.Quotient.factor`, the map `Rsh/I Rsh → Rsh/𝔪_{Rsh} = κ^{sep}`
of the inclusion `I Rsh ≤ 𝔪_{Rsh}`; its hypothesis `hπRsh` is then `rfl`, it is surjective by
`Ideal.Quotient.factor_surjective`, and `IsLocalRing.ker_eq_maximalIdeal` identifies `ker π` with
the maximal ideal of `Rsh/I Rsh`.

Three further facts of independent interest, none of them in Mathlib, are what remains.

*A local ring is its own localization at any prime equal to its maximal ideal*:
`Stacks.StrictHenselization.QuotientDescent.isLocalization_atPrime_of_eq_maximalIdeal`. It is used
three times — for `R`, for `R/I`, and for `Rsh/I Rsh` at `ker π` — which is why it is stated for a
prime that is *equal to* the maximal ideal rather than for `maximalIdeal R` itself.

*A quotient of a local ring by a proper ideal is local*: `isLocalRing_quotient`. Both quotients of
the statement are local by this lemma, `I` being proper because `I ≤ 𝔪`, and `I Rsh` because
`I Rsh ≤ 𝔪 Rsh = 𝔪_{Rsh}`.

*A quotient square is a pushout square*:
`Stacks.Henselian.isPushout_quotient_map` says that `R/I → S/IS` is the
base change of `R → R/I` along `R → S`, i.e. `S ⊗_R R/I = S/IS`. It is deduced from
`Ideal.qoutMapEquivTensorQout`, which is the same isomorphism of `S`-modules, by
`Algebra.IsPushout.symm`.

## References

* [StacksProject 05WS](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05WS)
* [StacksProject 05WR](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05WR), of which this is the case
  `S = R/I`
* [StacksProject 04GP](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/04GP), the properties spelling out a
  strict henselization
-/

namespace Stacks.StrictHenselization.QuotientDescent



open IsLocalRing

/-- A local ring is its own localization at any prime equal to its maximal ideal. -/
private theorem isLocalization_atPrime_of_eq_maximalIdeal {R : Type*} [CommRing R] [IsLocalRing R]
    (p : Ideal R) [p.IsPrime] (hp : p = maximalIdeal R) : IsLocalization.AtPrime R p :=
  IsLocalization.self fun _ hx => notMem_maximalIdeal.mp (hp ▸ hx)

/-- The quotient of a local ring by an ideal contained in its maximal ideal is local. -/
private theorem isLocalRing_quotient_of_le_maximalIdeal {A : Type*} [CommRing A] [IsLocalRing A]
    {J : Ideal A} (hJ : J ≤ maximalIdeal A) : IsLocalRing (A ⧸ J) :=
  isLocalRing_quotient (ne_top_of_le_ne_top (maximalIdeal.isMaximal A).ne_top hJ)

/-- **A separable algebraic closure of `K` is one of every field isomorphic to `K`.** Let `M` be a
separable algebraic closure of a field `K` and let `e : K ≃+* L` be an isomorphism of fields. Then
`M` is a separable algebraic closure of `L` for the structure map transported along `e`, which is
returned together with the identity `(L → M) ∘ e = (K → M)` that pins it down.

This is the separable analogue of `IsAlgClosed.of_ringEquiv`: `IsSepClosed M` speaks of `M` alone,
so all that moves is the separability of `M` over the base, by
`Algebra.IsSeparable.of_equiv_equiv`. -/
public theorem exists_isSepClosure_of_ringEquiv {K L M : Type*} [Field K] [Field L] [Field M]
    [Algebra K M] [IsSepClosure K M] (e : K ≃+* L) :
    ∃ _ : Algebra L M, IsSepClosure L M ∧
      (algebraMap L M).comp (e : K →+* L) = algebraMap K M := by
  let _ : Algebra L M := ((algebraMap K M).comp e.symm.toRingHom).toAlgebra
  have hsq : (algebraMap L M).comp (e : K →+* L) = algebraMap K M := by
    rw [RingHom.algebraMap_toAlgebra ((algebraMap K M).comp e.symm.toRingHom)]
    ext x
    simp
  exact ⟨_, isSepClosure_iff.mpr ⟨IsSepClosure.sep_closed K,
    Algebra.IsSeparable.of_equiv_equiv e (.refl M) (by simpa using hsq)⟩, hsq⟩

/-- A surjective local ring map between local rings induces an isomorphism on residue fields. -/
public theorem residueField_map_bijective_of_surjective {A B : Type*} [CommRing A] [CommRing B]
    [IsLocalRing A] [IsLocalRing B] (f : A →+* B) [IsLocalHom f] (hf : Function.Surjective f) :
    Function.Bijective (ResidueField.map f) := by
  refine ⟨(ResidueField.map f).injective, fun y => ?_⟩
  obtain ⟨s, rfl⟩ := residue_surjective y
  obtain ⟨r, rfl⟩ := hf s
  exact ⟨residue A r, ResidueField.map_residue f r⟩

/-- **A separable algebraic closure of `κ(A)` is one of `κ(B)`, for `A → B` a surjective ring map
of local rings.** This is `exists_isSepClosure_of_ringEquiv` for the isomorphism
`κ(A) ≅ κ(B)` of `residueField_map_bijective_of_surjective`, with the identity pinning the
embedding `κ(B) → M` down read as a square over `f`: `κ(B) → M` after `B → κ(B)` after `f` is
`κ(A) → M` after `A → κ(A)`. Surjectivity is the only hypothesis on `f`; that `f` is local
follows, by `IsLocalHom.of_surjective`. -/
private theorem exists_isSepClosure_residueField_of_surjective {A B M : Type*} [CommRing A]
    [CommRing B] [IsLocalRing A] [IsLocalRing B] [Field M] [Algebra (ResidueField A) M]
    [IsSepClosure (ResidueField A) M] (f : A →+* B) (hf : Function.Surjective f) :
    ∃ _ : Algebra (ResidueField B) M, IsSepClosure (ResidueField B) M ∧
      (algebraMap (ResidueField B) M).comp ((residue B).comp f) =
        (algebraMap (ResidueField A) M).comp (residue A) := by
  have : IsLocalHom f := IsLocalHom.of_surjective f hf
  obtain ⟨_, hsep, hsq⟩ := exists_isSepClosure_of_ringEquiv (M := M)
    (RingEquiv.ofBijective _ (residueField_map_bijective_of_surjective f hf))
  exact ⟨_, hsep, RingHom.ext fun x => by
    simpa [ResidueField.map_residue] using RingHom.congr_fun hsq (residue A x)⟩

open CategoryTheory in
/-- **Strict henselization is compatible with quotients** (Stacks 05WS). Let `(R, 𝔪, κ)` be a
local ring and let `R → Rsh` be a strict henselization of `R`. Let `I ⊆ 𝔪` be an ideal. Then
`Rsh/I Rsh` is a strict henselization of `R/I`. -/
public theorem solution {R Rsh : Type u} [CommRing R]
    [CommRing Rsh] [IsLocalRing R] [Algebra R Rsh] [HenselianLocalRing Rsh]
    [IsLocalHom (algebraMap R Rsh)] [IsSepClosure (ResidueField R) (ResidueField Rsh)]
    (hind : (CommAlgCat.etale.{u} R).ind.{u} (CommAlgCat.of R Rsh))
    (hmax : (maximalIdeal R).map (algebraMap R Rsh) = maximalIdeal Rsh)
    (I : Ideal R) (hI : I ≤ maximalIdeal R) :
    ∃ (_ : IsLocalRing (R ⧸ I)) (_ : IsLocalRing (Rsh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rsh)))
        (_ : IsLocalHom (algebraMap (R ⧸ I) (Rsh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rsh)))),
      HenselianLocalRing (Rsh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rsh)) ∧
      (CommAlgCat.etale.{u} (R ⧸ I)).ind.{u}
        (CommAlgCat.of (R ⧸ I) (Rsh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rsh))) ∧
      (maximalIdeal (R ⧸ I)).map (algebraMap (R ⧸ I) (Rsh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rsh))) =
        maximalIdeal (Rsh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rsh)) ∧
      IsSepClosure (ResidueField (R ⧸ I))
        (ResidueField (Rsh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rsh))) := by
  have hJle : I.map (algebraMap R Rsh) ≤ maximalIdeal Rsh := hmax ▸ Ideal.map_mono hI
  have hlocR := isLocalRing_quotient_of_le_maximalIdeal hI
  have hlocRsh := isLocalRing_quotient_of_le_maximalIdeal hJle
  have : IsLocalHom (algebraMap R (R ⧸ I)) :=
    IsLocalHom.of_surjective _ Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective
  have : (maximalIdeal (R ⧸ I)).LiesOver (maximalIdeal R) :=
    ⟨(maximalIdeal_comap (algebraMap R (R ⧸ I))).symm⟩
  have : IsLocalization.AtPrime R (maximalIdeal R) :=
    isLocalization_atPrime_of_eq_maximalIdeal _ rfl
  have : IsLocalization.AtPrime (R ⧸ I) (maximalIdeal (R ⧸ I)) :=
    isLocalization_atPrime_of_eq_maximalIdeal _ rfl
  -- `κ^{sep}` is a separable algebraic closure of the residue field of `R ⧸ I` too, and the square
  -- it comes with is the hypothesis `hπSq` of Lemma 05WR
  obtain ⟨_, _, hres⟩ := exists_isSepClosure_residueField_of_surjective (M := ResidueField Rsh)
    (algebraMap R (R ⧸ I)) Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective
  -- the map `π` of Lemma 05WR is the one `Rsh/I Rsh → Rsh/𝔪_{Rsh}` of the inclusion `I Rsh ≤ 𝔪`
  let π : (Rsh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rsh)) →+* ResidueField Rsh := Ideal.Quotient.factor hJle
  have : (RingHom.ker π).IsPrime := RingHom.ker_isPrime π
  have : IsLocalization.AtPrime (Rsh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rsh)) (RingHom.ker π) :=
    isLocalization_atPrime_of_eq_maximalIdeal _
      (ker_eq_maximalIdeal π (Ideal.Quotient.factor_surjective hJle))
  have hπRsh : π.comp (algebraMap Rsh (Rsh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rsh))) =
      (algebraMap (ResidueField Rsh) (ResidueField Rsh)).comp (residue Rsh) :=
    RingHom.ext fun _ => rfl
  have hπSq : π.comp (algebraMap (R ⧸ I) (Rsh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rsh))) =
      (algebraMap (ResidueField (R ⧸ I)) (ResidueField Rsh)).comp (residue (R ⧸ I)) :=
    Ideal.Quotient.ringHom_ext <| RingHom.ext fun x =>
      (ResidueField.algebraMap_residue x).symm.trans (RingHom.congr_fun hres x).symm
  obtain ⟨hlh, -, -, hhens, hindq, hmaxq, hsep, -, -, -⟩ :=
    Algebra.IsPushout.strictHenselization_and_finite_of_quasiFiniteAt
      (R := R) (S := R ⧸ I) (Rp := R) (Sq := R ⧸ I) (Rsh := Rsh)
      (T := Rsh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rsh)) (Ssh := Rsh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rsh))
      (κ₂ := ResidueField Rsh) (maximalIdeal R) (maximalIdeal (R ⧸ I)) hind hmax
      (Stacks.Henselian.isPushout_quotient_map I) π hπRsh hπSq
  exact ⟨hlocR, hlocRsh, hlh, hhens, hindq, hmaxq, hsep⟩

end Stacks.StrictHenselization.QuotientDescent

/-!
# Strict henselization is compatible with quotients

Let `(R, 𝔪, κ)` be a local ring, let `R → Rsh` be a strict henselization of `R`, and let `I ⊆ 𝔪`
be an ideal of `R`. Then the quotient `Rsh/I Rsh` is a strict henselization of the quotient `R/I`:
it is a strictly henselian local ring, it is a filtered colimit of étale `R/I`-algebras, the ideal
generated by the image of the maximal ideal of `R/I` is its maximal ideal, and its residue field is
a separable algebraic closure of the residue field `κ` of `R/I`.

Both the hypothesis "`Rsh` is a strict henselization of `R`" and the conclusion "`Rsh/I Rsh` is a
strict henselization of `R/I`" are spelled by the items of Lemma 04GP, which by the uniqueness of
Lemma 08HT pin a strict henselization down up to isomorphism over the base (Definition 04GQ(3)
declares any local ring map isomorphic to one of them to be a strict henselization, which is why
the source says "*a* strict henselization"). So this lemma transfers that package along the
quotient maps `R → R/I` and `Rsh → Rsh/I Rsh`.

## Main results

* `IsLocalRing.strictHenselization_quotient`: the statement above.

## Implementation notes

*A strict henselization is the package of Lemma 04GP.* The hypotheses `HenselianLocalRing Rsh`,
`IsSepClosure κ κ_sh`, `hind`, `hmax` (together with the `Algebra R Rsh` and `IsLocalHom` binders)
are the items (1)–(5) of that lemma, and the conclusion is the same package for
`R/I → Rsh/I Rsh`; "`Λ` is a filtered colimit of étale `A`-algebras" is
`(CommAlgCat.etale A).ind (CommAlgCat.of A Λ)` here as in `IsLocalRing.exists_henselization`
(04GN). The hypotheses and the conclusion are exactly the data of
`Stacks.StrictHenselization.IsStrictHenselization`, so bundling them would give
`IsStrictHenselization.quotient` with one instance on each side; they are kept apart here because
only these five items are used. This is the strict analogue of
`IsLocalRing.henselization_quotient` (05WQ) and is stated in the same shape.

*Items (2) and (5) are one class.* `IsSepClosure (ResidueField R) (ResidueField Rsh)` says that
the residue field extension induced by `R → Rsh` exhibits `κ_sh` as a separable algebraic closure
of `κ`: its field `IsSepClosure.sep_closed` is `IsSepClosed (ResidueField Rsh)`, which together
with `HenselianLocalRing Rsh` is this repository's spelling of "`Rsh` is strictly henselian" (item
(2)), and its field `IsSepClosure.separable`
is item (5), `κ_sh = κ^{sep}` for a separable algebraic closure `κ ⊆ κ^{sep}`. Splitting the two
apart would only restate the same conjunction. The source's item (1), that `R^h → R^{sh}` is
local, is a statement about the intermediate henselization, which does not occur here; what this
statement needs of it — that `R → Rsh` is local — is the `IsLocalHom` binder.

*The `R/I`-algebra structure on `Rsh/I Rsh`.* It is Mathlib's instance
`Ideal.Quotient.algebraQuotientMapQuotient`, induced by `R → Rsh`, and the identity
`algebraMap (R ⧸ I) (Rsh ⧸ I Rsh) (Ideal.Quotient.mk I x) =
Ideal.Quotient.mk _ (algebraMap R Rsh x)` holds by `rfl`. So the square formed by the two
quotient maps and `R → Rsh` commutes by construction and carries no compatibility hypothesis.

*The existentially bound instances.* `IsLocalRing (R ⧸ I)`, `IsLocalRing (Rsh ⧸ I Rsh)` and the
locality of `R/I → Rsh/I Rsh` are part of the conclusion, not assumptions, and they are bound
ahead of the conjuncts because `IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal`, `IsLocalRing.ResidueField` and the
algebra structure on residue fields take them as instance arguments. All three are `Prop`s, so
binding them existentially chooses no data; this is the device used in the statements of 04GN and
05WQ.

*The hypothesis on `I`.* `I ≤ maximalIdeal R` is the source's `I ⊂ 𝔪_R`, and for a local ring it
is equivalent to `I ≠ ⊤` (`IsLocalRing.le_maximalIdeal`). It cannot be dropped: for `I = ⊤` both
quotients are the zero ring and none of the conjuncts holds.

Faithfulness to [StacksProject 05WS], point by point. (1) No hypothesis is dead: each of
`HenselianLocalRing Rsh`, `IsSepClosure`, `hind`, `hmax` is used by the conjunct of the conclusion
carrying the same item number, the `IsLocalHom` binder by the locality of the induced map, and
`hI` by the two `IsLocalRing` conjuncts. (2) The scalars and the base are the source's: the ideal
one divides by is `I Rsh = I.map (algebraMap R Rsh)`, the ideal of `Rsh` generated by the image of
`I`, and `Rsh/I Rsh` is treated as an algebra over `R/I` — not over `R`, and not over `R` modulo
the contraction of `I Rsh` — so the colimit, the maximal ideal and the residue field extension of
the conclusion are all over the base `R/I`. (3) The canonical maps are pinned: the structure map
of `Rsh/I Rsh` over `R/I` is the induced quotient map, the third conjunct speaks of `Ideal.map`
along it, and the `IsSepClosure` conjunct is taken for the algebra structure on residue fields
induced by that very map (`IsLocalRing.ResidueField.algebraMap_residue`), so no unrelated
embedding of `κ` into the residue field of `Rsh/I Rsh` satisfies it. (4) The instantiation is not
degenerate: for a separably closed field `K` all hypotheses hold with `R = Rsh = K` and `I = ⊥`
(`Field.henselian`; `K` is étale over `K`, hence ind-étale by
`CategoryTheory.ObjectProperty.le_ind`; and `IsSepClosure (ResidueField K) (ResidueField K)`
because `ResidueField K` is module-finite over `K`, hence algebraic, so separably closed —
compiled); and the conclusion is not automatic: for `R = ℤ` localized at `(p)`, whose strict
henselization has residue field `𝔽_p^{sep} ≠ 𝔽_p`, and `I = p² R`, it asserts that
`Rsh/p² Rsh` is a strict henselization of `ℤ/p²`, so that both the presentation as a filtered
colimit of étale algebras and the separably closed residue field survive the quotient.

